@page rh="HOUSE AUREM · HANNAH · STANDING" m="TKC: TL · TR · CR · FR 26–29; VOL. I · {v1:arrival} · {v1:declaration}"
# The clerk's version and the true one
rec:
On the ledger | House Mother. She travels to the Crownward with her Covenant Daughter and two Appointed Officers (TR), and she's staff across many Trials, never the House's owner (FR 26).
In fact | She knows the back staircases and the camera gaps, and always has (TL). She doesn't let herself feel things she can't act on, and has built a precise system for acting on everything that matters (TL).
What a career costs | FR 26 makes her twelve a career count, which "ages her in the work." Every one of the twelve was married down the ladder or went to the heir (FR 19, 25).
What she can do for Naomi | Hannah can submit Naomi's name, and she isn't the only one who can. Naomi can self-nominate once she's eligible, the way Eden did. Hannah's power is influence and not legal control (FR 27).
What decides it | If Eden wins, Hannah enters Naomi, because winning proves the ladder can be climbed. If Eden dies or is publicly rejected, Hannah gets her out, and the only exit is a designation lower than the Trials reach (FR 29).
prompt: What happened to each of her twelve girls. Name the ones you can. | fill

@page rh="HOUSE AUREM · HANNAH · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE · 1 OF 2" m="TKC: TL · FR 30 · CR; VOL. I · {v1:temple} · {v1:teaches} · {v1:lived6}"
# What she believes
rec:
Who taught her | The Temple, as a Vowed woman (FR 30). The Crownward's Vowed are trained to perform devotion under close observation, and to hold what they know in memory instead of on paper (CR).
What she believes | INFERENCE: Act on everything that matters, and feel nothing you can't act on (TL). OPEN QUESTION: Whether she believes in God, the Temple, or only in the system she's built.
What she can recite | INFERENCE: Everything a Vowed woman is formed in, and everything a House Mother says on camera.
What she'd be arrested for | INFERENCE: What she knows about the camera gaps, and whatever she does with them.
empire: What the Temple teaches about a woman like her
A House Mother serves the House, and the House "always judges fairly" (DR3). POSSIBLE: The Temple preaches 1 Samuel 1.28, "I have lent him to the LORD," at every House Mother's commissioning, so handing over a girl who isn't hers becomes the highest kind of motherhood. It never preaches what Hannah does with her own.
/end
prompt: What she prays, if she prays, the night before a Declaration | fill

@page rh="HOUSE AUREM · HANNAH · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE · 2 OF 2" m="1 SAM 1–2; 1 SAM 1.15; 1 SAM 1.28; 1 SAM 2.4–8; ESTH 2.8–9, 15; JER 38.7–13"
# The text
rec:
Her name | Hannah, the mother of Samuel, who prays at Shiloh with no sound and is taken for drunk, vows her son to the sanctuary, and brings him a little robe every year. Her song is the model for Mary's (Vol. I, {v1:hannah}).
Where it breaks | POSSIBLE: The story runs in reverse. Biblical Hannah gives the son she prayed for. This Hannah gives other women's daughters every year and is deciding whether to give her own (FR 29, Vol. I, {v1:hannah}).
Hegai · Esther 2 | POSSIBLE: The keeper of the women in the king's house, who prefers Esther, gives her what she needs, and whose advice she follows: she "required nothing but what Hegai… appointed" (2.15). **Where it breaks:** Hegai's job ends at the king's door. Hannah's girls are hers to place, to lose, and to watch married down.
Ebed-melech · Jeremiah 38 | POSSIBLE: A palace servant who gets the king's permission and pulls a prophet out of a cistern with ropes and rags (Vol. I, {v1:echoes}). **Where it breaks:** Ebed-melech acts once, in the open. Hannah's whole method is the camera gaps.
tab:
| She'd quote | Never read to her |
| 1 Samuel 1.15, "I am a woman of a sorrowful spirit," if she ever said it aloud | 1 Samuel 2.4–8, the bows of the mighty broken and the poor raised from the dust |
| _ | Esther 2.15, where the girl wins by taking the keeper's advice |
